{"title":"1977 IMO Problems","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Problems of the 19th <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO\">IMO</a> 1977 in Yugoslavia.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Day_I\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Day I</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Problem_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problem_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Problem_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 3</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Day_II\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Day II</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Problem_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Problem_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Problem_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 6</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Day_I\">Day I</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Day I\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_1\">Problem 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>In the interior of a square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> we construct the equilateral triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/1/911c4eb6a2f85d522f6dc928c5ca11f197b399cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABK, BCL, CDM, DAN.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"206\" height=\"16\" /> Prove that the midpoints of the four segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/feca780cbe3262ebc09df01b60ad44e89fad1e0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL, LM, MN, NK$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"18\" /> and the midpoints of the eight segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba49bdcb2051a81c2b5322033cc132586ca6a955.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AK, BK, BL, CL, CM, DM, DN, AN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"298\" height=\"16\" /> are the 12 vertices of a regular dodecagon.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\">Problem 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>In a finite sequence of real numbers the sum of any seven successive terms is negative and the sum of any eleven successive terms is positive. Determine the maximum number of terms in the sequence.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\">Problem 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be a given number greater than 2. We consider the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> of all the integers of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/e/8de86497cfc157ecf7759d1c94d31c8b3ba23ea2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + kn$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/2/a6250e1c2ace5b7a875c446d32d372a91867a1f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1, 2, \\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" /> A number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> is called indecomposable in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> if there are not two numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/5/d457bcb364e21cf3fa0b23afb4ee0aa637354c9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m = pq.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"11\" /> Prove that there exist a number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/d/fadabce9d11979e64e9a4bf5eb2d5d121f2206b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r \\in V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> that can be expressed as the product of elements indecomposable in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> in more than one way. (Expressions which differ only in order of the elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> will be considered the same.)\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Day_II\">Day II</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Day II\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\">Problem 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/1/361f0c874212fed8be67cc782e4520b92ea12d87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,A,B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"69\" height=\"16\" /> be given reals. We consider the function defined by<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/792a9f8a71c29da6bd80b3305bc44e6b8ce0bdca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x) = 1 - a \\cdot \\cos(x) - b \\cdot \\sin(x) - A \\cdot \\cos(2x) - B \\cdot \\sin(2x).\\]\" width=\"468\" height=\"18\" />Prove that if for any real number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/3/12316c92a0c36c915d79988dc32c40578d1c0ce4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/f/fffba0b1a6e6d4b19503515f57ae34dd0aa6e0ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2 + b^2 \\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"90\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/9/c29d2c310b925fb291a303402562e4b65593a00b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A^2 + B^2 \\leq 1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"102\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_5\">Problem 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/b/e9bdf3e496517e8f8742f81dd774be1f93cf6e8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> be two natural numbers. When we divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/6/cf669d58fbd0410ca860758e53606d06425a4d97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2+b^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add2609af1bb7632cc22b68fd49fdd94de06b327.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"16\" />, we the the remainder <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and the quotient <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/f/9cf560f497c604f9cdc8256b4352f8d49b0a43f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"13\" /> Determine all pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/e/28e24c43456d95b5ab8d4f2f8f85a644e98b7ed3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a, b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/f/1df47f579e603ef6bc081ba58966b485ead3f57a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q^2 + r = 1977.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_6\">Problem 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e6cd0e4103015efba12e0fc4291f012b467eaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the set of positive integers. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> be a function defined on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e6cd0e4103015efba12e0fc4291f012b467eaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, which satisfies the inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/d/60d38f9067918a70ca3d58f00082044cc78f97f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n + 1) &gt; f(f(n))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/961d336c96250f23ace9c62514783e30511c69cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" />. Prove that for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/5/cd5d064761fa0ec9b4febecdc5a988047f837e6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n) = n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO\" title=\"1977 IMO\">1977 IMO</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=1&amp;cid=16&amp;year=1977\">IMO 1977 Problems on the Resources page</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions,_with_authors\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors\">IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics_competition_resources\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Mathematics competition resources\">Mathematics competition resources</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO\" title=\"1977 IMO\">1977 IMO</a></b> (<b><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO\" title=\"1976 IMO\">1976 IMO</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO\" title=\"1978 IMO\">1978 IMO</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018042431\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.029 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.051 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 227/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 837/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 56/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6814/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.671      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.671      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:20517:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018042431 and revision id 143958. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}